% ============================================================================
% ocean-current-strongest-location — tidal currents → where they are
% strongest, decoded from a span already sitting unused inside the SAME
% NOAA source `ocean-current-drivers.adj` already cites
% (adj-facts-stdlib, OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `ocean-current-drivers.adj`
% (`ocean_current_driver(current_type, driver)`, ONE physical driver per
% current type — tidal_currents → tides) and `ocean-current-surface-
% position.adj` (`ocean_current_surface_position(current_type, position)`,
% wind_driven_currents → at_or_near_the_oceans_surface): a new
% `ocean_current_strongest_location(current_type, location)` table names
% WHERE the SAME already-cited NOAA "What is a current?" page states
% tidal currents are strongest, decoded from a clause already sitting
% unused inside `ocean-current-drivers.adj`'s own header truth table --
% no new WebFetch. That table's schema has room for only the DRIVER; the
% already-quoted tidal-currents sentence also carries a location fact
% that never made it into a row:
%
%   tidal_currents → near_the_shore_and_in_bays_and_estuaries_along_the_coast
%     "Tides create a current in the oceans, which are strongest near the
%      shore, and in bays and estuaries along the coast."
%
% Both prepositional phrases ("near the shore" and "in bays and
% estuaries along the coast") are simultaneously true of where tidal
% currents are strongest — the source's own "and" joins two coexisting
% location facts, not two competing readings, so folding them into ONE
% compound atom is a faithful, unambiguous transcription (the same
% "and"-joined-clause-as-compound-atom convention already used elsewhere
% in this stdlib, e.g. `hurricane-category-home-damage.adj`).
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_current_strongest_location(tidal_currents, $Location)  % near_the_shore_and_in_bays_and_estuaries_along_the_coast
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like both
% siblings' own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `ocean_current_strongest_location(current_type, location)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the
% engine returns the location WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any current type that is not the one
% tabled here (it never invents a location). The query also runs
% BACKWARD -- bind a location and recall which current type is strongest
% there:
%
%     ? ocean_current_strongest_location($CurrentType, near_the_shore_and_in_bays_and_estuaries_along_the_coast)  % tidal_currents
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table, not a forced three-row one: neither
% the wind_driven_currents nor the thermohaline_circulation quote already
% sitting inside the parent table's own header names a comparably
% "strongest where" location fact for that current type --
% wind_driven_currents' cited span names a vertical surface POSITION
% (already captured by the sibling `ocean-current-surface-position.adj`,
% a different kind of fact), and thermohaline_circulation's cited span
% ("...density differences in water due to temperature (thermo) and
% salinity (haline) variations in different parts of the ocean.") names
% no location at all -- so this table correctly abstains on both rather
% than inventing one.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME tidal-currents span
% already quoted inside `ocean-current-drivers.adj`'s own
% header/provenance section (and already carried, verbatim, as that
% table's own `source` field for the tidal_currents row) -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of that
% already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the
% parent table already carries for this row (a primary U.S. government
% .gov source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_current_strongest_location {
    columns current_type, location

    row (tidal_currents, near_the_shore_and_in_bays_and_estuaries_along_the_coast)

    source "Tides create a current in the oceans, which are strongest near the shore, and in bays and estuaries along the coast."
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/current.html"
    trust authoritative
}
